      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Francisella tularensis—small Gram-negative coccobacillus, principally found in wild lagomorphs and rodents; facultative intracellular parasite that survives and grows in the liver, resulting in granulomas and/or abscesses:",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Type A is more virulent and found in",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "rabbits and ticks, type B is waterborne and found in rodents and ticks.",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "In United States most cases found in",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Missouri, Alaska, Oklahoma, South Dakota, Tennessee, Kansas, Colorado, Illinois, Utah, Maine, New York, and New Jersey.",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Increasing number of tularemia outbreaks",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "in regions of Europe outside the classic endemic areas in recent years.",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Absent from United Kingdom, Africa, South America, Australia.",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tularemia infection in cats follows a bimodal seasonal incidence curve with peaks in spring and late summer–fall, and may reflect the seasonality of the disease in the rabbit.",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Infection—ingestion of tissue or body fluids of an infected mammal or contaminated water, bitten by blood-sucking arthropod (tick), flies, mites, midges, fleas, or mosquitoes; few bacteria needed to infect cats through skin, airways, or conjunctiva; larger number required to infect through gastrointestinal tract:",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Skin contact—organism multiplies",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "locally (papule) 3–5 days after contact; ulcerates 2–4 days later; spreads via lymphatics to regional lymph node (LN) and bloodstream; results in septicemia (lung, liver, spleen, LN, bone marrow).",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Ingestion—may involve lymphadenopathy",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "of cervical and mesenteric LN followed by septicemic spread; distribution of lesions to face, oral cavity, tonsils, intestines, and LN.",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Acute disease—2–7 days after contact with organism.",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Has high aerosol-related infection rate, low infectious dose, and ability to induce fatal disease.",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "F.tularensisis considered a potential biologic warfare agent; occurrence of clusters of cases in companion animals may indicate potential human risk.",
              "pdf_page": 1441,
              "printed_page": 1359,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
